    "created_at": "2024-04-20T15:18:23Z",
    "updated_at": "2024-04-20T15:18:23Z",
    "body": "One of the advantages of having to go through an `<img>` element is that this allows for easier sandboxing of the SVG image. For instance [here I modified](https://jsfiddle.net/1smp2de6/) the original fiddle to show both the in DOM `<svg>` and the one drawn on the canvas. We wouldn't want the `:visited` selector to be leaked in the canvas where it can be read back.  \r\n\r\nSo this means that the browser can't go directly from the image rendered into the doc to the canvas, it will have to recompute it there, or to taint the canvas which puts quite a limit on the feature.  \r\n\r\nI'd personally propose that if we do this for `<svg>`, why not do it for *any* element? IIRC there was already a proposal for such an \"any element as source\" idea, though I can't find it right now.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
